From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: bq256xx: report device scope for the battery

The driver registers a power supply of type POWER_SUPPLY_TYPE_BATTERY that
only exposes the charge current and voltage limits, with no state of charge.
UPower still treats it as a system battery: it reads no capacity, assumes 0%,
and averages it with the real fuel gauge, so the composite battery shown in
the UI reads half of the true value.

Report POWER_SUPPLY_SCOPE_DEVICE, which is the standard way to tell userspace
this is not the system battery. UPower then leaves it out of the system
battery entirely. UPOWER_IGNORE via udev does not help here, because UPower
builds the composite device from the sysfs "scope" attribute before honouring
udev environment.

--- a/drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c
@@ -978,6 +978,16 @@
 		val->intval = bq->init_data.vbatreg_max;
 		break;
 
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_SCOPE:
+		/*
+		 * This power supply only carries the charge current and voltage
+		 * limits, not a state of charge. Report device scope so that
+		 * userspace (e.g. UPower) does not mistake it for the system
+		 * battery and average it with the real fuel gauge.
+		 */
+		val->intval = POWER_SUPPLY_SCOPE_DEVICE;
+		break;
+
 	default:
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
@@ -1224,6 +1234,7 @@
 static enum power_supply_property bq256xx_battery_props[] = {
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_CURRENT_MAX,
 	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_VOLTAGE_MAX,
+	POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_SCOPE,
 };
 
 static int bq256xx_property_is_writeable(struct power_supply *psy,
